OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026", "title": "The symmetric Mahler conjecture and its equality cases", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "087", "family_title": "The Mahler conjectures, functional inequalities and polar-product symplectic width", "abstract_html": "We resolve the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively, including its equality classification. Every origin-symmetric convex body in ℝ<sup><i>n</i></sup> has volume product at least \\(4^n/n!\\), with equality exactly for invertible linear images of Hanner polytopes.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A convex body in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) is a compact convex set with nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">For an origin-symmetric convex body \\(K=-K\\), define\n  \\[\n  K^\\circ=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle x,y\\rangle\\le 1\\text{ for every }x\\in K\\},\n  \\qquad\n  P(K)=|K|\\,|K^\\circ|,\n  \\]\n  where \\(|\\cdot|\\) denotes Lebesgue volume in the dimension of the body.</p><p class=\"li\">A centered nondegenerate interval is a one-dimensional Hanner polytope. If \\(H_1\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(H_2\\subset\\mathbb R^\\ell\\) are Hanner polytopes, then so are\n  \\[\n  H_1\\oplus_\\infty H_2=H_1\\times H_2\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  H_1\\oplus_1 H_2\n  =\n  \\operatorname{conv}\\bigl((H_1\\times\\{0\\})\\cup(\\{0\\}\\times H_2)\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A linear Hanner body is an invertible linear image of a Hanner polytope.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(n\\ge 1\\) and every origin-symmetric convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\),\n\\[\n|K|\\,|K^\\circ|\\ge \\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n\\]\nEquality holds if and only if \\(K\\) is a linear Hanner body. The linear equivalence respects the fixed origin used for polarity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 6 proves\n\\[\n|K|\\,|K^\\circ|\\ge\\frac{4^n}{n!}\n\\]\nfor every origin-symmetric convex body in every dimension.</p><p>Proposition 9 proves that equality implies that \\(K\\) is a linear Hanner body. Conversely, Proposition 1 proves that every \\(n\\)-dimensional linear Hanner body has volume product \\(4^n/n!\\). These statements establish the inequality and the complete equality classification.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2, “Even functional Mahler inequality.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge 1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\) be an even proper lower-semicontinuous convex function satisfying\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its Fenchel–Legendre transform is\n  \\[\n  \\varphi^*(y)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\{\\langle x,y\\rangle-\\varphi(x)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the convention \\(e^{-\\infty}=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx\\right)\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi^*(y)}\\,dy\\right)\n\\ge 4^n.\n\\]\nThe second integral may be infinite, in which case the inequality is immediate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(f=e^{-\\varphi}\\), a nonzero integrable log-concave function. For \\(z\\in\\mathbb R^n\\), Fradelizi and Meyer use the polar function\n\\[\nf^z(y)\n=\n\\inf_{\\{x:f(x)&gt;0\\}}\n\\frac{e^{-\\langle x-z,y-z\\rangle}}{f(x)}\n\\]\nand the translation-minimized functional volume product\n\\[\nP(f)\n=\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(x)\\,dx\\right)\n\\inf_{z\\in\\mathbb R^n}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f^z(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>Their Proposition 1(a), in “Some functional inverse Santaló inequalities,” Advances in Mathematics \\(218\\) (2008), cited as reference [7], states that the symmetric geometric Mahler inequality in every dimension implies\n\\[\nP(f)\\ge4^n\n\\]\nfor every even log-concave \\(f\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) with \\(0&lt;\\int f&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>Theorem 1 supplies this all-dimensional hypothesis. Since\n\\[\nf^0=e^{-\\varphi^*},\n\\]\nthe product in the statement is at least \\(P(f)\\), and hence at least \\(4^n\\).</p><p>The cited implication uses auxiliary convex bodies in dimensions \\(n+m\\) and lets \\(m\\to\\infty\\); it is not an application only of the geometric theorem in dimension \\(n\\).</p><p>The constant is sharp. For\n\\[\n\\varphi(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^n|x_i|,\n\\]\nthe transform \\(\\varphi^*\\) is zero on \\([-1,1]^n\\) and \\(+\\infty\\) outside, and both integrals equal \\(2^n\\). The limiting implication does not by itself classify the even potentials attaining equality; Theorem 1 classifies equality for convex bodies.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 1.3, “Symmetric entropy–transport inequality.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge 1\\), and let\n  \\[\n  \\eta_i(dx)=e^{-V_i(x)}\\,dx,\\qquad i=1,2,\n  \\]\n  be full-dimensional log-concave probability measures, both symmetric about the origin, with proper lower-semicontinuous convex potentials \\(V_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Their densities are essentially continuous: \\(e^{-V_i}=0\\) at \\(\\mathcal H^{n-1}\\)-almost every point of \\(\\partial\\operatorname{supp}\\eta_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define their moment measures by\n  \\[\n  \\nu_i=(\\nabla V_i)_\\#\\eta_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a probability measure \\(\\eta\\) with a density, define its entropy relative to Lebesgue measure by\n  \\[\n  H(\\eta)=\\int\\log\\left(\\frac{d\\eta}{dx}\\right)\\,d\\eta.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For probability measures \\(\\mu_1,\\mu_2\\) with finite first moments, set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)\n  =\n  \\inf_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\n  \\left\\{\\int f\\,d\\mu_1+\\int f^*\\,d\\mu_2\\right\\},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal F\\) is the class of proper lower-semicontinuous convex functions\n  \\(\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\), and \\(f^*\\) is the Fenchel–Legendre transform. The cost may equal \\(+\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measures \\(\\nu_i\\) have finite first moments, the quantities\n\\[\nH(\\eta_i)=-\\int V_i\\,d\\eta_i\n\\]\nare finite, and\n\\[\nH(\\eta_1)+H(\\eta_2)\n\\le -n\\log(4e^2)+\\mathcal T(\\nu_1,\\nu_2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\rho_i=e^{-V_i}\\), the identity\n\\[\n|\\nabla\\rho_i|\n=\n|\\nabla V_i|e^{-V_i}\n\\]\nholds almost everywhere on the interior of the support.</p><p>Lemma 4 and the proof of Proposition 7 of Cordero-Erausquin and Klartag, “Moment measures,” Journal of Functional Analysis \\(268\\) (2015), cited as reference [5], give\n\\[\n\\int|\\nabla\\rho_i|&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nV_i\\in L^1(\\eta_i),\n\\]\nrespectively. The pushforward definition of \\(\\nu_i\\) therefore gives its finite first moment, and\n\\[\nH(\\eta_i)=-\\int V_i\\,d\\eta_i\n\\]\nis finite.</p><p>Gozlan’s equivalence between the symmetric functional inverse Santaló and entropy–transport inequalities, as stated in Fradelizi–Gozlan–Zugmeyer, “Transport proofs of some functional inverse Santaló inequalities,” Theorem 1.3, cited as reference [8], applies to Theorem 2 with \\(c=4\\). It yields\n\\[\nH(\\eta_1)+H(\\eta_2)\n\\le\n-n\\log(4e^2)+\\mathcal T(\\nu_1,\\nu_2).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Polarity, norm sums, and the attaining Hanner family.", "statement": "<p>Polarity, norm sums, and the attaining Hanner family.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All bodies in this proposition are origin-symmetric convex bodies.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the gauge and support function by\n  \\[\n  p_A(x)=\\inf\\{t&gt;0:x\\in tA\\},\n  \\qquad\n  h_A(y)=\\max_{x\\in A}\\langle x,y\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  The support-function notation also applies to any nonempty compact convex set.</p><p class=\"li\">Identify dual spaces with Euclidean coordinate spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(A'\\subset\\mathbb R^\\ell\\), define\n  \\[\n  A\\oplus_\\infty A'=A\\times A',\n  \\qquad\n  A\\oplus_1 A'\n  =\n  \\operatorname{conv}\\bigl((A\\times\\{0\\})\\cup(\\{0\\}\\times A')\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The polarity and linear-invariance identities are\n\\[\nA^{\\circ\\circ}=A,\\qquad p_A=h_{A^\\circ},\\qquad h_A=p_{A^\\circ},\n\\]\nand, for every invertible linear map \\(T\\),\n\\[\n(TA)^\\circ=T^{-T}A^\\circ,\\qquad P(TA)=P(A).\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\in\\{1,\\infty\\}\\),\n\\[\nP(A\\oplus_s A')\n=\n\\frac{P(A)P(A')}{\\binom{k+\\ell}{k}}.\n\\]\nConsequently every \\(d\\)-dimensional linear Hanner body \\(H\\) satisfies\n\\[\nP(H)=\\frac{4^d}{d!},\n\\]\nand its polar is again a linear Hanner body.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 proves the polarity, linear-invariance, and gauge identities. Lemma 1.2 proves the norm-sum volume-product formula. Lemma 1.3 applies that formula recursively to obtain the Hanner volume product and closure under polarity.</p><p>In dimension one every origin-symmetric convex body is a centered nondegenerate interval, so both the sharp inequality and its equality classification follow directly from the interval base case.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Polarity and the two sum gauges; these are the preliminary identities in Section 2.", "statement": "<p>Polarity and the two sum gauges; these are the preliminary identities in Section 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(A'\\subset\\mathbb R^\\ell\\) be origin-symmetric convex bodies, with gauges, support functions, and sums as defined in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be an invertible linear map on the ambient space of \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nA^{\\circ\\circ}=A,\\qquad p_A=h_{A^\\circ},\\qquad h_A=p_{A^\\circ},\n\\]\n\\[\n(TA)^\\circ=T^{-T}A^\\circ,\\qquad P(TA)=P(A),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\np_{A\\oplus_\\infty A'}(x,x')\n=\n\\max\\{p_A(x),p_{A'}(x')\\},\n\\]\n\\[\np_{A\\oplus_1 A'}(x,x')\n=\np_A(x)+p_{A'}(x').\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The gauge is a norm with closed unit ball \\(A\\). Convex separation gives the bipolar identity \\(A^{\\circ\\circ}=A\\). Since \\(x\\in tA\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle\\le t\\qquad(y\\in A^\\circ),\n\\]\nwe obtain \\(p_A=h_{A^\\circ}\\), and applying this to \\(A^\\circ\\) gives \\(h_A=p_{A^\\circ}\\).</p><p>For an invertible linear map \\(T\\), testing the polar condition gives\n\\[\n(TA)^\\circ=T^{-T}A^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe volumes acquire reciprocal determinant factors, so \\(P(TA)=P(A)\\).</p><p>The product gauge is the maximum of the coordinate gauges. For the \\(\\ell_1\\) sum, a convex combination from the two coordinate bodies has gauge sum at most one. Conversely, when the sum of the two gauges is at most one, use these gauges as the coefficients of the normalized coordinate vectors and place any remaining weight at \\(0\\). A zero coordinate contributes no term.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Polars and volumes of the two sums.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(A\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(A'\\subset\\mathbb R^\\ell\\) be origin-symmetric convex bodies, where \\(k,\\ell\\ge 1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(A\\oplus_1 A')^\\circ=A^\\circ\\oplus_\\infty(A')^\\circ,\n\\qquad\n(A\\oplus_\\infty A')^\\circ=A^\\circ\\oplus_1(A')^\\circ.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n|A\\oplus_\\infty A'|=|A|\\,|A'|,\n\\qquad\n|A\\oplus_1 A'|\n=\n\\frac{k!\\ell!}{(k+\\ell)!}|A|\\,|A'|.\n\\]\nConsequently, for \\(s\\in\\{1,\\infty\\}\\),\n\\[\nP(A\\oplus_s A')\n=\n\\frac{P(A)P(A')}{\\binom{k+\\ell}{k}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Testing a functional on the two coordinate bodies gives the first polar identity; taking polars again gives the second.</p><p>The product volume follows from Fubini. By Lemma 1.1, the fiber of \\(A\\oplus_1 A'\\) over \\(x\\in A\\) is\n\\[\n(1-p_A(x))A'.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n|\\{x:p_A(x)\\le t\\}|=t^k|A|,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1,\n\\]\nintegration gives\n\\[\n|A\\oplus_1 A'|\n=\nk|A|\\,|A'|\\int_0^1t^{k-1}(1-t)^\\ell\\,dt\n=\n\\frac{k!\\ell!}{(k+\\ell)!}|A|\\,|A'|.\n\\]\nThe last integral is evaluated by repeated integration by parts. Combining the polar and volume identities proves the formula for the volume product.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “The Hanner volume product.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>A Hanner polytope is constructed from centered nondegenerate intervals by finitely many \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_\\infty\\) sums. A linear Hanner body is an invertible linear image of a Hanner polytope.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(d\\)-dimensional linear Hanner body \\(H\\) satisfies\n\\[\nP(H)=\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]\nThe polar of a linear Hanner body is again a linear Hanner body.</p>", "proof": "<p>A centered interval \\([-a,a]\\), \\(a&gt;0\\), has polar \\([-1/a,1/a]\\) and volume product \\(4\\). Lemma 1.2 propagates \\(4^k/k!\\) and \\(4^\\ell/\\ell!\\) to\n\\[\n\\frac{4^{k+\\ell}}{(k+\\ell)!}\n\\]\nunder either sum. Induction and linear invariance prove the first assertion.</p><p>The polar identities exchange the two operations and preserve the interval base case. The identity\n\\[\n(TH)^\\circ=T^{-T}H^\\circ\n\\]\nthen proves closure under polarity also for linear images.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The conformal lens and its endpoint scaling.", "statement": "<p>The conformal lens and its endpoint scaling.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb D=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F(z)=\\frac{8}{\\pi^2}\n  \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^jz^{2j+1}}{(2j+1)^2}.\n  \\]\n  This series converges uniformly on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(F\\) is a biholomorphism from \\(\\mathbb D\\) onto a bounded convex domain \\(D\\), with holomorphic inverse \\(g\\), and \\(F'(0)=8/\\pi^2\\).</p><p>There is a continuous even function\n\\[\n\\lambda:[-1,1]\\to[0,\\infty),\n\\]\npositive on \\((-1,1)\\), zero at \\(\\pm1\\), and smooth on \\((-1,1)\\), such that\n\\[\n\\overline D=\\{v+it:|t|\\le1,\\ |v|\\le\\lambda(t)\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lambda'(t)\n=\n-\\frac2\\pi\\operatorname{arctanh}\\left(\\sin\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda''(t)=-\\sec\\frac{\\pi t}{2}&lt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Theta\\) is uniform on \\([0,2\\pi)\\), then\n\\[\nF(e^{i\\Theta})\\stackrel{\\mathrm{law}}=\n\\varepsilon\\lambda(T)+iT,\n\\]\nwhere \\(T\\) is uniform on \\([-1,1]\\), and \\(\\varepsilon\\) is an independent uniform sign.</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\n\\lambda(1-\\eta)\n=\n\\frac2\\pi\\eta\\log\\frac1\\eta+O(\\eta)\n\\qquad(\\eta\\downarrow0),\n\\]\nand for every \\(0&lt;a\\le b&lt;\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{a\\le c\\le b}\n\\left|\n\\frac{\\lambda(1-\\delta c)}{\\lambda(1-\\delta)}-c\n\\right|\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(\\delta\\downarrow0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves the biholomorphic convex lens, its half-width formulas, and the uniform boundary-coordinate law. Lemma 2.2 proves the endpoint asymptotic and its uniform ratio form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “The planar lens.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\mathbb D\\) and \\(F\\) be as in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(F\\) is a biholomorphism from \\(\\mathbb D\\) onto a bounded convex domain \\(D\\), has a holomorphic inverse \\(g\\), and satisfies \\(F'(0)=8/\\pi^2\\). There is a continuous even function \\(\\lambda:[-1,1]\\to[0,\\infty)\\), positive on \\((-1,1)\\), zero at \\(\\pm1\\), and smooth on \\((-1,1)\\), such that\n\\[\n\\overline D=\\{v+it:|t|\\le1,\\ |v|\\le\\lambda(t)\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lambda'(t)\n=\n-\\frac2\\pi\\operatorname{arctanh}\\left(\\sin\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda''(t)=-\\sec\\frac{\\pi t}{2}&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor a uniform angle \\(\\Theta\\),\n\\[\nF(e^{i\\Theta})\\stackrel{\\mathrm{law}}=\n\\varepsilon\\lambda(T)+iT,\n\\]\nwhere \\(T\\) is uniform on \\([-1,1]\\), and \\(\\varepsilon\\) is an independent uniform sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map is odd and commutes with conjugation. Put\n\\[\nw=\\arctan z=\\frac1{2i}\\log\\frac{1+iz}{1-iz},\n\\qquad z\\in\\mathbb D.\n\\]\nThe fraction inside the logarithm lies in the right half-plane, where the principal logarithm is used. Thus\n\\[\n|\\Re w|&lt;\\frac\\pi4,\\qquad \\tan w=z,\n\\]\nand \\(w=0\\) only when \\(z=0\\).</p><p>Differentiating the series gives\n\\[\nF'(z)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\frac wz,\n\\qquad\n1+\\frac{zF''(z)}{F'(z)}\n=\n\\frac{\\sin(2w)}{2w},\n\\]\nwith their limiting values at zero. In particular \\(F'\\) has no zeros. If \\(w=a+ib\\ne0\\), the real part of the last expression is\n\\[\n\\frac{\na\\sin(2a)\\cosh(2b)+b\\cos(2a)\\sinh(2b)\n}{\n2(a^2+b^2)\n}&gt;0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(|a|&lt;\\pi/4\\); its value at zero is \\(1\\).</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\) and put\n\\[\n\\gamma_r(\\theta)=F(re^{i\\theta}).\n\\]\nThis curve is regular, and its tangent angle \\(\\beta\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\beta'(\\theta)\n=\n\\Re\\left(1+\\frac{zF''(z)}{F'(z)}\\right)&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\beta(\\theta+2\\pi)=\\beta(\\theta)+2\\pi.\n\\]\nThe second identity uses the zero-free derivative in the disk.</p><p>At a fixed parameter \\(\\theta_0\\), project the curve onto its right unit normal there. The derivative of this projection has the sign of\n\\[\n-\\sin(\\beta(\\theta)-\\beta(\\theta_0)).\n\\]\nIt is negative until the tangent has turned through \\(\\pi\\), and positive thereafter, until the curve returns to \\(\\theta_0\\). Hence the projection has its unique maximum at \\(\\theta_0\\).</p><p>This proves simplicity and shows that every point of the curve is the unique supporting point of its convex hull in the corresponding normal direction. All normal directions occur, so the curve is the boundary of that convex hull and is positively oriented. The harmonic maximum principle for these supporting projections places \\(F(r\\mathbb D)\\) strictly inside the curve. The argument principle then gives exactly one preimage for every point inside and none outside. Thus \\(F\\) maps \\(r\\mathbb D\\) biholomorphically onto its bounded convex interior \\(D_r\\).</p><p>As \\(r\\) increases, these domains exhaust \\(D=F(\\mathbb D)\\). Consequently \\(F\\) is univalent on \\(\\mathbb D\\), \\(D\\) is convex, and its inverse \\(g\\) is holomorphic. For each \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\), continuity gives\n\\[\n\\overline{D_r}=F(r\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset D.\n\\]\nContinuity on the closed disk gives boundedness and\n\\[\n\\overline D=F(\\overline{\\mathbb D}).\n\\]\nNo boundary value \\(F(\\zeta)\\), \\(|\\zeta|=1\\), lies in \\(D\\): otherwise continuity of \\(g\\) there and\n\\[\ng(F(r\\zeta))=r\\zeta\n\\]\nwould give \\(g(F(\\zeta))=\\zeta\\notin\\mathbb D\\). It follows that\n\\[\n\\partial D=F(\\partial\\mathbb D).\n\\]</p><p>On the right semicircle, write \\(z=e^{is}\\), where \\(-\\pi/2&lt;s&lt;\\pi/2\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{1+iz}{1-iz}\n=\n\\frac{i\\cos s}{1+\\sin s},\n\\qquad\n\\Re w=\\frac\\pi4,\n\\qquad\n\\Im w=\\frac12\\operatorname{arctanh}(\\sin s).\n\\]\nThe formulas for \\(w\\) and \\(F'\\) extend analytically across each compact subarc, so the boundary values may be differentiated. Writing\n\\[\nF(e^{is})=v(s)+it(s)\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nt'(s)=\\frac2\\pi,\n\\qquad\nv'(s)=-\\frac4{\\pi^2}\\operatorname{arctanh}(\\sin s).\n\\]\nSince \\(t(0)=0\\), one has \\(t(s)=2s/\\pi\\). Define\n\\[\n\\lambda(t)=v(\\pi t/2)\n\\]\nand extend it to \\([-1,1]\\) by continuity. Conjugation makes \\(\\lambda\\) even. The series shows that \\(F(\\pm i)\\) are purely imaginary, so \\(\\lambda(\\pm1)=0\\). Differentiation gives the asserted formulas for \\(\\lambda'\\) and \\(\\lambda''\\); strict concavity and the endpoint values give positivity in the interior.</p><p>Oddness and conjugation show that the other semicircle traces \\(v=-\\lambda(t)\\). These two graphs give the description of \\(\\overline D\\). On either semicircle the imaginary coordinate traverses \\([-1,1]\\) at constant absolute speed \\(2/\\pi\\). Each semicircle has probability \\(1/2\\), proving the boundary law.</p><p>The two boundary graphs are smooth away from \\(\\pm i\\) and have strictly turning tangent directions. A fixed tangent-line direction therefore occurs at only finitely many parameters away from these endpoints. The boundary has planar Lebesgue measure zero, also directly from its graph description.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Endpoint scaling.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\lambda\\) be the lens half-width in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda(1-\\eta)\n=\n\\frac2\\pi\\eta\\log\\frac1\\eta+O(\\eta).\n\\]\nFor every \\(0&lt;a\\le b&lt;\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{a\\le c\\le b}\n\\left|\n\\frac{\\lambda(1-\\delta c)}{\\lambda(1-\\delta)}-c\n\\right|\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(\\delta\\downarrow0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The formula for \\(\\lambda'\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{arctanh}(\\cos x)=\\log\\cot(x/2)\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\lambda(1-\\eta)\n=\n\\frac2\\pi\\int_0^\\eta\\log\\cot\\frac{\\pi s}{4}\\,ds.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\log\\cot(\\pi s/4)=\\log(1/s)+O(1)\n\\]\nuniformly as \\(s\\downarrow0\\). Integration proves the first assertion.</p><p>Writing \\(L=\\log(1/\\delta)\\), the same uniform remainder gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda(1-\\delta c)}{\\lambda(1-\\delta)}\n=\nc\\,\\frac{L-\\log c+O(1)}{L+O(1)}\n=\nc+O_{a,b}(L^{-1})\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(a\\le c\\le b\\). This proves the second assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Holomorphic mass.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(N\\ge d\\), and let \\(U\\subset\\mathbb C^d\\) be open and contain \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_N):U\\to\\mathbb C^N\\) be holomorphic.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume:\n  \\[\n  f^{-1}(0)=\\{0\\};\n  \\]\n  for an integer \\(k\\ge1\\),\n  \\[\n  f(z)=H(z)+O(|z|^{k+1})\n  \\quad\\text{near }0,\n  \\]\n  where every component of \\(H\\) is a homogeneous polynomial of degree \\(k\\), and \\(H(z)\\ne0\\) whenever \\(z\\ne0\\); and, for every \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  \\{z\\in U:|f(z)|^2\\le s\\}\n  \\]\n  is compact in \\(U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All norms are Euclidean. Write \\(z_j=x_j+iy_j\\), and use the complex orientation\n  \\[\n  dV=dx_1\\wedge dy_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx_d\\wedge dy_d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For smooth real functions, use\n  \\[\n  d^c=\\frac i4(\\bar\\partial-\\partial),\n  \\qquad\n  dd^c=\\frac i2\\partial\\bar\\partial.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(\\tau=|f|^2\\). Rows in each determinant below are ordered increasingly.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_{\\{\\tau&lt;1\\}}\n\\sum_{\\substack{I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\\\|I|=d}}\n\\left|\n\\det\\left(\\frac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial z_j}\\right)_\n{i\\in I,\\ 1\\le j\\le d}\n\\right|^2\\,dV\n\\ge\n\\frac{(\\pi k)^d}{d!}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>With the stated normalization,\n\\[\nd^cv(\\xi)=-\\frac14\\,dv(i\\xi),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ndd^c|z|^2=\\sum_{j=1}^d dx_j\\wedge dy_j,\n\\qquad\n(dd^cv)^d=d!\\det(v_{\\bar z_i z_j})\\,dV.\n\\]\nThe second identity follows by expanding the exterior product, or by a unitary diagonalization of the Hermitian Hessian.</p><p>First bound the integral of \\((dd^c\\tau)^d\\). Both \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\log\\tau\\), away from \\(0\\), have positive semidefinite complex Hessians. Indeed, if \\(Z\\) is the complex derivative matrix of \\(f\\), the Hessian of \\(\\tau\\) is \\(Z^*Z\\). In a complex direction \\(\\xi\\), the Hessian of \\(\\log\\tau\\) evaluates to\n\\[\n\\frac{|f|^2|df(\\xi)|^2-|\\langle df(\\xi),f\\rangle|^2}{|f|^4}\\ge0\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz. Their top exterior powers are therefore nonnegative.</p><p>Fix a regular value \\(s\\in(0,1)\\), and set\n\\[\nE_s=\\{z\\in U:\\tau(z)&lt;s\\}.\n\\]\nCompactness of the sublevel and the regular-value theorem permit Stokes’ theorem:\n\\[\n\\int_{E_s}(dd^c\\tau)^d\n=\n\\int_{\\partial E_s}d^c\\tau\\wedge(dd^c\\tau)^{d-1}.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\gamma=d^c\\log\\tau\n\\]\non \\(U\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). The identities\n\\[\nd^c\\log\\tau=\\tau^{-1}d^c\\tau,\n\\]\n\\[\ndd^c\\log\\tau\n=\n\\tau^{-1}dd^c\\tau-\\tau^{-2}d\\tau\\wedge d^c\\tau\n\\]\nand the vanishing of the pullback of \\(d\\tau\\) to \\(\\partial E_s\\) imply\n\\[\ns^{-d}\\int_{E_s}(dd^c\\tau)^d\n=\n\\int_{\\partial E_s}\\gamma\\wedge(d\\gamma)^{d-1}.\n\\]</p><p>For sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the closed Euclidean ball \\(\\overline{B_\\varepsilon}\\) lies in \\(E_s\\). Orient \\(S_\\varepsilon=\\partial B_\\varepsilon\\) outward from that ball. Stokes on \\(E_s\\setminus\\overline{B_\\varepsilon}\\) gives\n\\[\ns^{-d}\\int_{E_s}(dd^c\\tau)^d\n-\n\\int_{S_\\varepsilon}\\gamma\\wedge(d\\gamma)^{d-1}\n=\n\\int_{E_s\\setminus\\overline{B_\\varepsilon}}\n(dd^c\\log\\tau)^d\n\\ge0.\n\\]</p><p>It remains to determine the flux through a shrinking sphere. Let\n\\[\nT(z)=|H(z)|^2,\\qquad S=\\{z:|z|=1\\}.\n\\]\nThe holomorphic Taylor expansion gives\n\\[\n\\varepsilon^{-2k}\\tau(\\varepsilon z)\\longrightarrow T(z)\n\\]\nin \\(C^2\\) on a fixed closed annulus containing \\(S\\). Termwise differentiation of the convergent Taylor series gives this convergence for the rescaled map and then for its squared norm. Since \\(T\\) is strictly positive on that annulus, the logarithms converge in \\(C^2\\) as well. Pulling back to \\(S\\) by dilation, the additive constant \\(2k\\log\\varepsilon\\) disappears under \\(d^c\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\int_{S_\\varepsilon}\\gamma\\wedge(d\\gamma)^{d-1}\n=\n\\int_S\\alpha\\wedge(d\\alpha)^{d-1},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha=d^c\\log T\\big|_S.\n\\]</p><p>Homogeneity gives\n\\[\nT(rz)=r^{2k}T(z)\\quad(r&gt;0),\n\\qquad\nT(e^{i\\theta}z)=T(z).\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\log T-k\\log|z|^2\\) is unchanged by dilation and common phase rotation. On \\(S\\), put\n\\[\n\\alpha_0=d^c\\log|z|^2\\big|_S,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\beta=d^c(\\log T-k\\log|z|^2)\\big|_S,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_t=k\\alpha_0+t\\beta,\\quad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\alpha_1=\\alpha\\). For the nowhere-zero tangent vector field \\(V(z)=iz\\), dilation invariance and the normalization of \\(d^c\\) give\n\\[\n\\beta(V)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_0(V)=\\frac12.\n\\]\nPhase invariance makes every \\(\\alpha_t\\) invariant under the flow of \\(V\\). Cartan’s identity therefore gives\n\\[\n\\iota_Vd\\alpha_t\n=\n\\mathcal L_V\\alpha_t-d(\\alpha_t(V))\n=\n0.\n\\]\nThe top-degree form\n\\[\n\\beta\\wedge(d\\alpha_t)^{d-1}\n\\]\non the \\((2d-1)\\)-dimensional sphere vanishes: its contraction with the nonzero vector \\(V\\) is zero. Differentiation and integration by parts on the closed sphere yield\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt}\\int_S\\alpha_t\\wedge(d\\alpha_t)^{d-1}\n=\nd\\int_S\\beta\\wedge(d\\alpha_t)^{d-1}\n=\n0.\n\\]\nFor \\(d=1\\), this is simply the derivative of \\(\\int_S\\alpha_t\\).</p><p>On the unit sphere, \\(\\alpha_0\\) and \\(d\\alpha_0\\) are the restrictions of \\(d^c|z|^2\\) and \\(dd^c|z|^2\\). A final application of Stokes gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_S\\alpha\\wedge(d\\alpha)^{d-1}\n&amp;=\nk^d\\int_S\\alpha_0\\wedge(d\\alpha_0)^{d-1}\\\\\n&amp;=\nk^d\\int_{\\{|z|&lt;1\\}}(dd^c|z|^2)^d\\\\\n&amp;=\nk^d d!\\operatorname{vol}_{2d}(\\{|z|&lt;1\\})\\\\\n&amp;=(\\pi k)^d.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe comparison with the shrinking sphere consequently gives\n\\[\n\\int_{E_s}(dd^c\\tau)^d\\ge s^d(\\pi k)^d\n\\]\nfor every regular \\(s\\in(0,1)\\). Sard’s theorem supplies an increasing sequence of such values tending to \\(1\\). Nonnegativity and monotone convergence imply\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{\\tau&lt;1\\}}(dd^c\\tau)^d\\ge(\\pi k)^d.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, the complex Hessian \\(Z^*Z\\) and Cauchy–Binet give\n\\[\n(dd^c\\tau)^d\n=\nd!\\sum_{|I|=d}|\\det Z_I|^2\\,dV.\n\\]\nDivision by \\(d!\\) proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Boundary feasibility.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge1\\), and let \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\) be nonzero real row vectors spanning \\((\\mathbb R^d)^*\\). Extend their action complex-linearly to \\(\\mathbb C^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) be the collection of \\(d\\)-element sets of independent rows. For \\(I\\in\\mathcal B\\), let \\(B_I=(b_i)_{i\\in I}\\), with rows in increasing order.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F:\\mathbb D\\to D\\) be any biholomorphism onto a bounded planar domain, continuous on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\), with \\(F(0)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For independent uniform angles \\(\\theta_i\\in[0,2\\pi)\\), \\(i\\in I\\), define\n  \\[\n  Z_I(\\theta)=B_I^{-1}\\bigl(F(e^{i\\theta_i})\\bigr)_{i\\in I},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  P_I\n  =\n  \\mathbb P\\{b_jZ_I(\\theta)\\in\\overline D\\text{ for all }j\\},\n  \\qquad\n  S=\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}P_I.\n  \\]\n  The active coordinates \\(i\\in I\\) already belong to \\(\\overline D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nS\\ge1.\n\\]\nNo condition on extra-row boundary equalities or on dependent row subsets is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(g=F^{-1}\\), and set\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^d:b_jz\\in D\\text{ for every }j\\},\n\\qquad\nh_j(z)=g(b_jz).\n\\]\nThe set \\(\\Omega\\) is open, bounded, and contains \\(0\\).</p><p>For an integer \\(k\\ge1\\), apply Proposition 3 to\n\\[\nf_k=(h_1^k,\\ldots,h_m^k),\n\\qquad\n\\tau_k=\\sum_{j=1}^m|h_j|^{2k}.\n\\]\nThe unique zero of \\(f_k\\) is \\(0\\), because \\(g\\) vanishes only at \\(0\\), and the rows span. Writing \\(c=g'(0)\\ne0\\), its degree-\\(k\\) leading term is\n\\[\n((cb_1z)^k,\\ldots,(cb_mz)^k),\n\\]\nwhich is nonzero whenever \\(z\\ne0\\).</p><p>For \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\), the set \\(\\{\\tau_k\\le s\\}\\) is compact in \\(\\Omega\\). Indeed, each row coordinate belongs to the fixed compact set\n\\[\nF(\\{|w|\\le s^{1/(2k)}\\})\\subset D.\n\\]\nA basis of rows bounds \\(z\\); limits retain all these inclusions and the inequality \\(\\tau_k\\le s\\).</p><p>The derivative row of \\(h_j^k\\) is a scalar multiple of \\(b_j\\), so dependent row subsets have zero minors. Proposition 3 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}M_{I,k}\n\\ge\nk^d\\frac{\\pi^d}{d!},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nM_{I,k}\n=\n\\int_{\\{\\tau_k&lt;1\\}}\n\\left|\n\\det\\frac{\\partial(h_i^k)_{i\\in I}}\n{\\partial(z_1,\\ldots,z_d)}\n\\right|^2\\,dV_{2d}(z).\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(I\\in\\mathcal B\\). The change of variables\n\\[\nu_i=h_i(z),\\qquad i\\in I,\n\\]\nis one-to-one onto its image, with inverse\n\\[\nz=B_I^{-1}(F(u_i))_{i\\in I}.\n\\]\nIts squared complex Jacobian is its real volume Jacobian. The squared determinant in \\(M_{I,k}\\) is this Jacobian factor times\n\\[\nk^{2d}\\prod_{i\\in I}|u_i|^{2k-2}.\n\\]\nThe transformed integration domain is contained in\n\\[\n\\left\\{\nu\\in\\mathbb D^d:\n\\sum_{i\\in I}|u_i|^{2k}&lt;1,\\\nb_jB_I^{-1}(F(u_i))_{i\\in I}\\in D\\text{ for all }j\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nUse this larger domain for an upper bound, then put\n\\[\nu_i=\\rho_i^{1/k}e^{i\\theta_i}.\n\\]\nThe coordinate hyperplanes have Lebesgue measure zero. Since\n\\[\nk^2|u_i|^{2k-2}\\,dV_2(u_i)\n=\nk\\rho_i\\,d\\rho_i\\,d\\theta_i,\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{M_{I,k}}{k^d}\n\\le\n\\int_{\\substack{\\rho_i&gt;0,\\ \\sum_i\\rho_i^2&lt;1\\\\\n\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)^d}}\n&amp;\\mathbf1\\left\\{\nb_jB_I^{-1}\n\\bigl(F(\\rho_i^{1/k}e^{i\\theta_i})\\bigr)_{i\\in I}\n\\in D\\text{ for every }j\n\\right\\}\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{25mm}\\cdot\\prod_{i\\in I}\\rho_i\\,d\\rho_i\\,d\\theta_i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe measure in this integral is finite, with total mass \\(\\pi^d/d!\\), the volume of the unit ball in \\(\\mathbb C^d\\).</p><p>For every positive \\(\\rho_i\\), continuity of \\(F\\) on the closed disk makes the inferred points converge to \\(Z_I(\\theta)\\). If a limiting row coordinate lies outside \\(\\overline D\\), that constraint fails for all sufficiently large \\(k\\). The upper limit of the indicator is therefore bounded by the indicator of the event defining \\(P_I\\), including all possible boundary equalities.</p><p>The upper-bound form of Fatou’s lemma, applied to functions bounded by \\(1\\) on this finite measure space, gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{M_{I,k}}{k^d}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\pi^d}{d!}P_I.\n\\]\nThere are finitely many bases, so\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\pi^d}{d!}\n&amp;\\le\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\n\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}\\frac{M_{I,k}}{k^d}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{M_{I,k}}{k^d}\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{\\pi^d}{d!}\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}P_I.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus \\(S\\ge1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, “Feasible-simplex identity.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge1\\), and let \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\) be nonzero, pairwise nonproportional real row vectors spanning \\((\\mathbb R^d)^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  A=\\{X\\in\\mathbb R^d:|b_jX|\\le1\\text{ for every }j\\},\n  \\qquad\n  C=A^\\circ=\\operatorname{conv}\\{\\pm b_1,\\ldots,\\pm b_m\\}.\n  \\]\n  Rows are identified with vectors when taking convex hulls.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F,D,\\lambda\\) be the lens map, domain, and half-width of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B\\), \\(B_I\\), \\(P_I\\), and \\(S\\) be as in Proposition 4, and put \\(D_I=|\\det B_I|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(X\\in\\operatorname{int}A\\), define\n  \\[\n  L_X=\\{Y\\in\\mathbb R^d:|b_jY|\\le\\lambda(b_jX)\\text{ for every }j\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(I\\in\\mathcal B\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}^I\\), put\n  \\[\n  Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X)\n  =\n  B_I^{-1}\\bigl(\\varepsilon_i\\lambda(b_iX)\\bigr)_{i\\in I}.\n  \\]\n  Call \\((I,\\varepsilon)\\) feasible at \\(X\\) if \\(Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X)\\in L_X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  T_{I,\\varepsilon}\n  =\n  \\operatorname{conv}\\bigl(0,\\{\\varepsilon_i b_i:i\\in I\\}\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Sigma_X\n  =\n  \\bigcup_{\\substack{(I,\\varepsilon)\\\\\n  \\text{feasible at }X}}T_{I,\\varepsilon}.\n  \\]\n  On \\(\\partial A\\), declare every pair infeasible and set \\(\\Sigma_X=\\varnothing\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n1\\le S\n=\n\\frac{d!}{4^d}\\int_A|\\Sigma_X|\\,dX\n\\le\n\\frac{d!}{4^d}|A|\\,|A^\\circ|.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n|A|\\,|A^\\circ|\\ge\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\int_A\\bigl(|A^\\circ|-|\\Sigma_X|\\bigr)\\,dX\n=\n|A|\\,|A^\\circ|-\\frac{4^d}{d!}S,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n0\\le\n\\int_A\\bigl(|A^\\circ|-|\\Sigma_X|\\bigr)\\,dX\n\\le\n|A|\\,|A^\\circ|-\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The spanning assumption makes \\(A\\) bounded, and \\(0\\in\\operatorname{int}A\\). The polar identity follows from the bipolar theorem, because the polar of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{conv}\\{\\pm b_1,\\ldots,\\pm b_m\\}\n\\]\nis exactly \\(A\\).</p><p>All widths defining \\(L_X\\) are strictly positive, so \\(L_X\\) contains a neighborhood of \\(0\\). A basis of rows shows that it is bounded. It is therefore a convex polytope with nonempty interior. Each feasible pair specifies a vertex \\(Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X)\\). Every simplex in \\(\\Sigma_X\\) lies in \\(C=A^\\circ\\).</p><p>First convert each probability into a real integral. By Proposition 2, each active boundary coordinate has law\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_i\\lambda(t_i)+it_i,\n\\]\nwhere \\(t_i\\) is uniform on \\([-1,1]\\), and \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) is an independent fair sign. Each fixed sign choice therefore has joint measure\n\\[\n4^{-d}\\,dt_I\n\\]\non \\([-1,1]^d\\). Writing the inferred complex vector as \\(Y+iX\\) gives\n\\[\nt_I=B_IX,\\qquad Y=Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X).\n\\]\nThe conditions that all row coordinates lie in \\(\\overline D\\) are exactly\n\\[\nX\\in A,\\qquad Y\\in L_X,\n\\]\napart from the immaterial boundary \\(\\partial A\\). This boundary is null, being contained in the finitely many hyperplanes \\(b_jX=\\pm1\\). The change of variables \\(t_I=B_IX\\) gives\n\\[\nP_I\n=\n\\frac{D_I}{4^d}\n\\int_A\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}^I}\n\\mathbf1\\{(I,\\varepsilon)\\text{ is feasible at }X\\}\\,dX.\n\\]</p><p>For almost every \\(X\\in\\operatorname{int}A\\), every feasible pair has no tight constraint outside its basis. An additional equality means that an extra row of \\(Y+iX\\) lies on \\(\\partial D\\). Lemma 5.1 makes this event null in the basis phases; the boundary law and the invertible change \\(t_I=B_IX\\) make its set of \\(X\\) null for each fixed sign choice. There are finitely many bases, sign choices, and extra rows, so a single null set removes all these equalities.</p><p>Fix \\(X\\) outside this null set. Distinct feasible pairs have distinct vectors \\(Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X)\\). If their bases differ, a shared vector would have a tight row outside one of the bases. If the bases agree but some signs differ, a shared vector would satisfy opposite equations with the same strictly positive width, which is impossible.</p><p>The interiors of their simplices are disjoint. If\n\\[\ne\\in\\operatorname{int}T_{I,\\varepsilon},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\ne=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\alpha_i\\varepsilon_i b_i,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_i&gt;0,\\qquad \\sum_{i\\in I}\\alpha_i&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor every \\(Y\\in L_X\\),\n\\[\neY\\le\\sum_{i\\in I}\\alpha_i\\lambda(b_iX),\n\\]\nwith equality at \\(Y_{I,\\varepsilon}(X)\\). Equality forces every independent equation\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_i b_iY=\\lambda(b_iX),\n\\]\nso this is the unique maximizer of the linear functional \\(e\\) on \\(L_X\\). If \\(e\\) belonged to the interiors of two feasible simplices, their distinct vertices would both be its unique maximizer, a contradiction.</p><p>Each simplex has volume \\(D_I/d!\\), and its boundary has measure zero. Therefore, for almost every \\(X\\),\n\\[\nd!|\\Sigma_X|\n=\n\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal B}\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}^I}\nD_I\\,\\mathbf1\\{(I,\\varepsilon)\\text{ is feasible at }X\\}.\n\\]</p><p>The integrals are measurable. Feasibility for each pair is a finite collection of closed inequalities between continuous functions on \\(\\operatorname{int}A\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\{(X,e):X\\in\\operatorname{int}A,\\ e\\in\\Sigma_X\\}\n\\]\nis a finite union of products of Borel feasibility sets and fixed closed simplices. Fubini makes \\(X\\mapsto|\\Sigma_X|\\) measurable, and it is bounded by \\(|C|\\).</p><p>Summing the probability formula and using the simplex-volume formula gives\n\\[\nS=\\frac{d!}{4^d}\\int_A|\\Sigma_X|\\,dX.\n\\]\nProposition 4 gives \\(S\\ge1\\), while \\(\\Sigma_X\\subset C\\) gives\n\\[\nS\\le\\frac{d!}{4^d}|A|\\,|C|.\n\\]\nThe missing-volume identity follows:\n\\[\n\\int_A(|C|-|\\Sigma_X|)\\,dX\n=\n|A|\\,|C|-\\frac{4^d}{d!}S.\n\\]\nContainment makes it nonnegative, and \\(S\\ge1\\) gives the asserted upper bound.</p><p>For the paper’s concrete illustration of positive missing volume, take\n\\[\nb_1=(1,0),\\qquad\nb_2=(1/2,\\sqrt3/2),\\qquad\nb_3=b_2-b_1,\n\\qquad\nX=0.9(1,1/\\sqrt3).\n\\]\nThen \\(b_1X=b_2X=0.9\\), \\(b_3X=0\\). Put \\(a=\\lambda(0.9)\\). The endpoint integral for \\(\\lambda\\), together with \\(\\cot x&lt;1/x\\) for \\(0&lt;x&lt;\\pi/2\\), gives\n\\[\na&lt;\n\\frac{\\log(40/\\pi)+1}{5\\pi}\n&lt;\n\\frac4{15}.\n\\]\nThe alternating series for \\(F(1)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\lambda(0)&gt;\n\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\left(1-\\frac19\\right)&gt;\n\\frac{32}{45}.\n\\]\nThus \\(2a&lt;\\lambda(0)\\), so the third strip is strictly redundant. Here\n\\[\n\\Sigma_X=\\operatorname{conv}\\{\\pm b_1,\\pm b_2\\},\n\\]\nand the two uncovered caps in \\(C\\) have total area \\(\\sqrt3/2\\), one third of \\(|C|\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Additional boundary equalities have probability zero.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the nonzero, spanning, pairwise nonproportional rows and the lens map of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(I\\in\\mathcal B\\), let\n  \\[\n  Z_I(\\theta)=B_I^{-1}(F(e^{i\\theta_i}))_{i\\in I},\n  \\]\n  with independent uniform angles.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(I\\in\\mathcal B\\) and \\(j\\notin I\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{b_jZ_I(\\theta)\\in\\partial D\\}=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nb_jB_I^{-1}=(c_i)_{i\\in I}.\n\\]\nAt least two coefficients, say \\(c_a,c_b\\), are nonzero; otherwise \\(b_j\\) would be proportional to a basis row. In dimension one the reduced list has one row, so there are no extra-row equalities.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\gamma(\\theta)=F(e^{i\\theta}).\n\\]\nApart from its two endpoints, the lens boundary consists of the smooth regular branches\n\\[\n(\\pm\\lambda(t),t),\\qquad -1&lt;t&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor any fixed unoriented line direction, their tangent directions have at most one occurrence on each branch, because \\(\\lambda'\\) is strictly monotone. The boundary parameter has constant nonzero speed in \\(t\\) on each branch.</p><p>Hold all phases other than \\(\\theta_a,\\theta_b\\) fixed. The map\n\\[\n(\\theta_a,\\theta_b)\n\\longmapsto\nc_a\\gamma(\\theta_a)+c_b\\gamma(\\theta_b)\n+\\sum_{i\\ne a,b}c_i\\gamma(\\theta_i)\n\\]\nhas real Jacobian determinant\n\\[\n\\det(c_a\\gamma'(\\theta_a),c_b\\gamma'(\\theta_b)).\n\\]\nFor every nonendpoint \\(\\theta_a\\), it vanishes at only finitely many nonendpoint values of \\(\\theta_b\\). Its zero set, including the excluded endpoint lines, therefore has planar measure zero by Fubini’s theorem.</p><p>Off that set, the inverse function theorem gives local diffeomorphism charts. A countable subcover suffices, and in each chart the inverse is locally Lipschitz, so it sends a planar null set to a null set. The set \\(\\partial D\\) is planar-null, being a countable union of compact smooth subarcs and two points. Its inverse image consequently has measure zero in the two remaining phases. A final application of Fubini over the fixed phases proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Corollary 6.2, “The symmetric volume-product inequality.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), \\(d\\ge1\\), be an origin-symmetric convex body.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|K|\\,|K^\\circ|\\ge\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(Q=K^\\circ\\), and choose \\(a&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\naB_2^d\\subset Q,\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_2^d\\) is the Euclidean unit ball.</p><p>For \\(\\varepsilon_j\\downarrow0\\), take a finite \\(\\varepsilon_j\\)-net in \\(Q\\), adjoin its negatives, and let \\(Q_j\\) be its convex hull. Then \\(Q_j\\subset Q\\), and for every Euclidean unit vector \\(u\\),\n\\[\nh_Q(u)-\\varepsilon_j\\le h_{Q_j}(u)\\le h_Q(u),\n\\qquad\nh_Q(u)\\ge a.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\delta_j=\\varepsilon_j/a&lt;1\\), support-function comparison and polarity give\n\\[\n(1-\\delta_j)Q\\subset Q_j\\subset Q,\n\\]\n\\[\nK\\subset A_j:=Q_j^\\circ\\subset(1-\\delta_j)^{-1}K.\n\\]\nFor large \\(j\\), the bodies \\(Q_j,A_j\\) are full-dimensional symmetric polytopes.</p><p>Choose one vertex from each antipodal vertex pair of \\(Q_j\\) as a row list. These rows are nonzero and span. They are pairwise nonproportional: two distinct boundary vertices on the same ray from an interior origin cannot both be extreme, and the antipodal duplication has been removed. Their strip body is \\(A_j\\). Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n|A_j|\\,|Q_j|\\ge\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]</p><p>The homothetic bounds imply\n\\[\n|Q_j|\\longrightarrow|Q|,\n\\qquad\n|A_j|\\longrightarrow|K|,\n\\]\nbecause dilation by \\(t&gt;0\\) multiplies volume by \\(t^d\\). Passing to the limit proves the assertion. No smoothness, simplicity, or general-position assumption is imposed on \\(K\\).</p><p>For a general finite strip presentation, the row reduction used above causes no change to its body: discard zero rows, and on each row line retain a row of largest magnitude, whose strip implies all discarded strips on that line.</p><p>In dimension one, the inequality can also be read directly from\n\\[\nK=[-a,a],\\qquad K^\\circ=[-1/a,1/a],\\qquad P(K)=4.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, “A representation minimizing the lens cost.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=-K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), be a convex body satisfying\n  \\[\n  P(K)=\\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(Q=K^\\circ\\), \\(d=n+1\\), and\n  \\[\n  B=K\\oplus_1[-1,1]\n  =\n  \\{(x,s):p_K(x)+|s|\\le1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  C=B^\\circ=Q\\times[-1,1].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda\\) be the continuous even concave lens half-width of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(X\\in\\operatorname{int}B\\) and a nonzero vector\n  \\[\n  \\xi=(q,a)\\in\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R.\n  \\]\n  Define\n  \\[\n  T=p_C(\\xi)=\\max\\{p_Q(q),|a|\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(1\\le r\\le d\\), vectors\n\\[\nc_i=(b_i,\\sigma_i),\\qquad b_i\\in Q,\\quad \\sigma_i\\in\\{-1,1\\},\n\\]\nnonnegative coefficients \\(\\alpha_i\\), and \\(Y\\in\\mathbb R^d\\), such that\n\\[\n\\xi=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\alpha_i c_i,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^r\\alpha_i=T,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|h\\cdot Y|\\le\\lambda(h\\cdot X)\\qquad(h\\in C),\n\\]\n\\[\nc_i\\cdot Y=\\lambda(c_i\\cdot X)\\qquad(1\\le i\\le r).\n\\]\nIn particular, for every finite representation\n\\[\n\\xi=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\beta_jh_j,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_j\\ge0,\\quad h_j\\in C,\n\\]\nthe same chosen representation satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^r\\alpha_i\\lambda(c_i\\cdot X)\n\\le\n\\sum_{j=1}^s\\beta_j\\lambda(h_j\\cdot X).\n\\]\nNo independence of the selected vectors \\(c_i\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the prescribed \\(X\\) and nonzero \\(\\xi\\), put\n\\[\ne=\\xi/T\\in\\partial C.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 7.2 and set\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=T\\theta_i.\n\\]\nDiscard zero weights if desired. Since \\(\\xi\\ne0\\), at least one remains; there are at most \\(d\\). This gives\n\\[\n\\xi=\\sum_i\\alpha_i c_i,\\qquad \\sum_i\\alpha_i=T,\n\\]\ntogether with all the common-witness equalities and inequalities.</p><p>For any finite competing representation\n\\[\n\\xi=\\sum_j\\beta_jh_j,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_j\\ge0,\\quad h_j\\in C,\n\\]\nthe same witness gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\lambda(c_i\\cdot X)\n=\n\\xi\\cdot Y\n=\n\\sum_j\\beta_j h_j\\cdot Y\n\\le\n\\sum_j\\beta_j\\lambda(h_j\\cdot X).\n\\]\nThus the selected representation minimizes the lens cost against every positive representation with rows in \\(C\\). The conclusion includes directions for which every limiting representation is degenerate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Inner polytopes and vanishing missing volume; Section 7.1.", "statement": "<p>Inner polytopes and vanishing missing volume; Section 7.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the equality body \\(K\\), its polar \\(Q\\), and the lifted bodies \\(B,C\\) from Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q_j\\subset Q\\) be full-dimensional symmetric polytopes converging to \\(Q\\) in Hausdorff distance.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  C_j=Q_j\\times[-1,1],\n  \\qquad\n  B_j=C_j^\\circ.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Describe \\(B_j\\) by one row from each antipodal vertex pair of \\(C_j\\), and let \\(\\Sigma_{j,X}\\) be the feasible-simplex union of Proposition 5 for this row list.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(t_j\\in(0,1)\\), with \\(t_j\\to1\\), such that\n\\[\nt_jQ\\subseteq Q_j\\subseteq Q,\n\\]\n\\[\nt_jC\\subseteq C_j\\subseteq C,\n\\qquad\nB\\subseteq B_j\\subseteq t_j^{-1}B.\n\\]\nEvery signed row in the chosen strip description belongs to \\(Q_j\\times\\{-1,1\\}\\), and\n\\[\n|B|\\,|C|=\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n0\\le D_j:=\n\\int_{B_j}\\bigl(|C_j|-|\\Sigma_{j,X}|\\bigr)\\,dX\n\\le\n|B_j|\\,|C_j|-\\frac{4^d}{d!}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The horizontal section of \\(B\\) at height \\(s\\in[-1,1]\\) is \\((1-|s|)K\\). Hence\n\\[\n|B|\n=\n|K|\\int_{-1}^1(1-|s|)^n\\,ds\n=\n\\frac{2|K|}{n+1},\n\\qquad\n|C|=2|Q|.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n|B|\\,|C|\n=\n\\frac4{n+1}|K|\\,|Q|\n=\n\\frac{4^d}{d!}.\n\\]</p><p>The polytopes \\(Q_j\\) may be obtained as convex hulls of increasingly fine finite symmetric nets in \\(Q\\), including a fixed spanning symmetric set. If \\(Q\\) contains a Euclidean ball of radius \\(r_0&gt;0\\), and the Hausdorff error is \\(\\varepsilon_j\\), then for Euclidean unit directions,\n\\[\nh_{Q_j}\\ge h_Q-\\varepsilon_j\n\\ge\n(1-\\varepsilon_j/r_0)h_Q.\n\\]\nThe support-function criterion for containment gives \\(t_jQ\\subseteq Q_j\\subseteq Q\\), after discarding finitely many indices and choosing \\(t_j\\) slightly smaller if necessary. Thus\n\\[\nt_jC\\subseteq C_j\\subseteq C,\n\\qquad\nB\\subseteq B_j\\subseteq t_j^{-1}B.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n|B_j|\\,|C_j|\\longrightarrow|B|\\,|C|.\n\\]</p><p>The chosen rows span \\(\\mathbb R^d\\), and no two are proportional: a ray from the interior point \\(0\\) meets the boundary of a convex body in only one point. Every signed row is a vertex of \\(C_j\\), hence belongs to \\(Q_j\\times\\{-1,1\\}\\).</p><p>For every \\(X\\in\\operatorname{int}B_j\\), the set \\(\\Sigma_{j,X}\\) is a finite union of closed simplices contained in \\(C_j\\). Its definition is retained at exceptional interior points with several simultaneous tight constraints. Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n0\\le D_j\n\\le\n|B_j|\\,|C_j|-\\frac{4^d}{d!}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A common feasibility witness at every point and direction; Section 7.2.", "statement": "<p>A common feasibility witness at every point and direction; Section 7.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the equality body and lifted bodies of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q_j,C_j,B_j,t_j,\\Sigma_{j,X},D_j\\) be the inner-polytope construction of Lemma 7.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(X\\in\\operatorname{int}B\\) and \\(e\\in\\partial C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are vectors\n\\[\nc_i\\in Q\\times\\{-1,1\\},\\qquad 1\\le i\\le d,\n\\]\ncoefficients \\(\\theta_i\\ge0\\), and \\(Y\\in\\mathbb R^d\\), such that\n\\[\ne=\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_i c_i,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_i=1,\n\\]\n\\[\nc_i\\cdot Y=\\lambda(c_i\\cdot X)\\qquad(1\\le i\\le d),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|h\\cdot Y|\\le\\lambda(h\\cdot X)\\qquad(h\\in C).\n\\]\nThe limiting vectors \\(c_i\\) need not be independent.</p>", "proof": "<p>First construct indices \\(j_k\\to\\infty\\) and points\n\\[\nX_k\\in\\operatorname{int}B_{j_k},\n\\qquad\ne_k\\in\\Sigma_{j_k,X_k},\n\\qquad\n(X_k,e_k)\\longrightarrow(X,e).\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(k\\), choose a Euclidean ball \\(U_k\\) of positive volume contained in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{int}B\\cap B(X,1/k).\n\\]\nThere is also a ball \\(V_k\\) of positive volume whose closure is contained in\n\\[\n\\operatorname{int}C\\cap B(e,1/k).\n\\]\nTo see this at the boundary point \\(e\\), move a small distance from \\(e\\) toward \\(0\\); the resulting point is interior because \\(0\\in\\operatorname{int}C\\), and a sufficiently small ball works.</p><p>The inclusions in Lemma 7.1 give\n\\[\nU_k\\subset\\operatorname{int}B_j\n\\]\nfor every \\(j\\), and\n\\[\nV_k\\subset C_j\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(j\\). The latter also follows from\n\\[\n\\max_{h\\in\\overline{V_k}}p_C(h)&lt;1\n\\]\nand \\(t_j\\to1\\).</p><p>Choose \\(j_k&gt;j_{k-1}\\) so large that these inclusions hold and\n\\[\nD_{j_k}&lt;|U_k|\\,|V_k|.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Sigma_{j_k,Z}\\) missed \\(V_k\\) for every \\(Z\\in U_k\\), its missing volume would be at least \\(|V_k|\\) throughout \\(U_k\\), contradicting this inequality. Thus choose\n\\[\nX_k\\in U_k,\\qquad\ne_k\\in\\Sigma_{j_k,X_k}\\cap V_k.\n\\]\nThis proves the required convergence. The argument uses product neighborhoods, not a pointwise limit of \\(X\\mapsto|\\Sigma_{j,X}|\\).</p><p>Choose a feasible simplex containing \\(e_k\\). Its \\(d\\) signed basis rows\n\\[\nc_{1k},\\ldots,c_{dk}\\in Q_{j_k}\\times\\{-1,1\\}\n\\]\ngive weights\n\\[\ne_k=\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_{ik}c_{ik},\n\\qquad\n\\theta_{ik}\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_{ik}\\le1.\n\\]\nThe remaining coefficient is the weight of the simplex vertex \\(0\\). Feasibility supplies a vector \\(Y_k\\) satisfying\n\\[\nc_{ik}\\cdot Y_k=\\lambda(c_{ik}\\cdot X_k)\\qquad(1\\le i\\le d),\n\\]\n\\[\n|v\\cdot Y_k|\\le\\lambda(v\\cdot X_k)\n\\qquad\n\\text{for every vertex }v\\text{ of }C_{j_k}.\n\\]\nThe evenness of \\(\\lambda\\) absorbs the signs on basis rows.</p><p>The last inequality extends to every \\(h\\in C_{j_k}\\). Write \\(h=\\sum_\\ell s_\\ell v_\\ell\\) as a convex combination of vertices. The triangle inequality and concavity give\n\\[\n|h\\cdot Y_k|\n\\le\n\\sum_\\ell s_\\ell|v_\\ell\\cdot Y_k|\n\\le\n\\sum_\\ell s_\\ell\\lambda(v_\\ell\\cdot X_k)\n\\le\n\\lambda(h\\cdot X_k).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nY_k\\in\\lambda(0)B_{j_k}.\n\\]\nThe polar sandwich of Lemma 7.1 makes these witnesses uniformly bounded, even if the selected bases approach singularity.</p><p>Pass to a subsequence on which\n\\[\nY_k\\to Y,\\qquad c_{ik}\\to c_i,\\qquad\\theta_{ik}\\to\\theta_i\n\\]\nfor every \\(i\\). This uses a fixed finite product of compact sets: all rows lie in \\(Q\\times\\{-1,1\\}\\), and all weights lie in \\([0,1]\\). No independence of the limiting rows is needed. Therefore\n\\[\ne=\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_i c_i,\n\\qquad\n\\theta_i\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\theta_i\\le1.\n\\]\nContinuity gives\n\\[\nc_i\\cdot Y=\\lambda(c_i\\cdot X).\n\\]</p><p>For arbitrary \\(h\\in C\\), use\n\\[\nh_k=t_{j_k}h\\in C_{j_k}\n\\]\nin the feasibility inequality and pass to the limit:\n\\[\n|h\\cdot Y|\\le\\lambda(h\\cdot X).\n\\]\nThe subsequence has already been fixed, so this proves the inequality for every \\(h\\) with the same \\(Y\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(p_C(e)=1\\), so\n\\[\n1=p_C(e)\n\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_i p_C(c_i)\n\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^d\\theta_i\n\\le1.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\sum_i\\theta_i=1\\): no limiting mass remains at the simplex vertex \\(0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, “Medians of an equality body.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=-K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), be a convex body satisfying\n  \\[\n  P(K)=\\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(Q=K^\\circ\\), and use the norm \\(\\|z\\|=p_Q(z)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A metric median of \\(x_0,x_1,x_2\\) is a point \\(m\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\|x_i-x_j\\|\n  =\n  \\|x_i-m\\|+\\|m-x_j\\|\n  \\qquad(0\\le i&lt;j\\le2).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every triple of points in \\((\\mathbb R^n,p_Q)\\) has a metric median.</p>", "proof": "<p>Translation reduces the triple to \\(0,x,y\\). If two points coincide, the repeated point is a median, so assume \\(0,x,y\\) are distinct. Set\n\\[\np_1=\\|x\\|,\\qquad\np_2=\\|y\\|,\\qquad\np=\\|x-y\\|,\n\\]\n\\[\ns=p_1+p_2,\\qquad a=p_1-p_2.\n\\]\nAll three distances are positive. The triangle and reverse triangle inequalities give\n\\[\ns\\ge p\\ge|a|.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nA_+=\\frac{p+a}{2},\n\\qquad\nA_-=\\frac{p-a}{2},\n\\qquad\nc=\\frac{s-p}{2}.\n\\]\nThese are nonnegative and satisfy\n\\[\nA_++A_-=p,\\qquad\nc+A_+=p_1,\\qquad\nc+A_-=p_2.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nD_0=\n\\left\\{\nr_+-r_-:\nr_++r_-=x-y,\\\n\\|r_+\\|\\le A_+,\\\n\\|r_-\\|\\le A_-\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe goal is\n\\[\nx+y\\in D_0+(s-p)Q.\n\\]\nSuch membership supplies \\(r_+,r_-\\) for which\n\\[\nm=x-r_+=y+r_-\n\\]\nhas distances to \\(0,x,y\\) bounded by \\(c,A_+,A_-\\), respectively. Triangle inequalities will then force the median equalities.</p><p>The set \\(D_0\\) is compact and convex: it is the linear image of the intersection of \\(A_+Q\\times A_-Q\\) with the affine subspace \\(r_++r_-=x-y\\). It is nonempty because\n\\[\nr_\\pm=\\frac{A_\\pm}{p}(x-y)\n\\]\nsatisfy the constraints. This includes \\(A_+=0\\) or \\(A_-=0\\).</p><p>Fix \\(u\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\), and put\n\\[\nX_\\delta=(\\delta u,1-\\delta),\\qquad 0&lt;\\delta&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor the lifted body \\(B=K\\oplus_1[-1,1]\\),\n\\[\np_B(X_\\delta)\n=\n\\delta p_K(u)+1-\\delta&lt;1,\n\\]\nso \\(X_\\delta\\in\\operatorname{int}B\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 7 to \\(X_\\delta\\) and\n\\[\n(q,a)=(x-y,p_1-p_2).\n\\]\nThe total weight is\n\\[\nT=\\max\\{\\|q\\|,|a|\\}=p.\n\\]\nObtain rows \\((b_i,\\sigma_i)\\in Q\\times\\{-1,1\\}\\) and coefficients \\(\\alpha_i\\ge0\\) satisfying the representation and cost comparison of Proposition 7. Define\n\\[\nr_\\pm=\\sum_{\\sigma_i=\\pm1}\\alpha_i b_i.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nr_++r_-=x-y.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\sigma_i=\\pm1}\\alpha_i\n=\n\\frac{p\\pm a}{2}\n=\nA_\\pm.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\|r_\\pm\\|\\le A_\\pm\\), and\n\\[\nr_+-r_-\\in D_0.\n\\]\nNo choice of these representations across different \\(u\\) or \\(\\delta\\) is required.</p><p>For \\(b\\in Q\\) and \\(\\sigma=\\pm1\\), evenness of \\(\\lambda\\) gives\n\\[\n\\lambda(\\langle(b,\\sigma),X_\\delta\\rangle)\n=\n\\lambda\\bigl(1-\\delta(1-\\sigma\\langle b,u\\rangle)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe factors \\(1-\\sigma\\langle b,u\\rangle\\) lie in\n\\[\n[1-p_K(u),\\,1+p_K(u)],\n\\]\na compact subinterval of \\((0,\\infty)\\). Proposition 2 therefore supplies an error \\(\\varepsilon_u(\\delta)\\to0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\frac{\\lambda(\\langle(b,\\sigma),X_\\delta\\rangle)}\n{\\lambda(1-\\delta)}\n-1+\\sigma\\langle b,u\\rangle\n\\right|\n\\le\\varepsilon_u(\\delta)\n\\]\nfor all \\(b\\in Q\\), \\(\\sigma=\\pm1\\).</p><p>The normalized cost of the selected representation is consequently at least\n\\[\np-\\langle r_+-r_-,u\\rangle-p\\varepsilon_u(\\delta)\n\\ge\np-h_{D_0}(u)-p\\varepsilon_u(\\delta).\n\\]\nCompare this cost, using Proposition 7, with the two-term representation\n\\[\n(x-y,a)\n=\np_1(x/p_1,1)+p_2(-y/p_2,-1).\n\\]\nBoth rows lie in \\(Q\\times\\{-1,1\\}\\). Their normalized cost tends to\n\\[\ns-\\langle x+y,u\\rangle.\n\\]\nLetting \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\langle x+y,u\\rangle\n\\le\nh_{D_0}(u)+s-p\n\\qquad(u\\in\\operatorname{int}K).\n\\]\nThe error was uniform over \\(b,\\sigma\\) only for the fixed \\(u\\), which is all this limit requires.</p><p>Continuity extends the inequality to \\(u\\in K\\). For a nonzero \\(v\\in\\mathbb R^n\\), Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nu=\\frac{v}{h_Q(v)}\\in\\partial K.\n\\]\nHomogeneity of support functions yields\n\\[\n\\langle x+y,v\\rangle\n\\le\nh_{D_0}(v)+(s-p)h_Q(v).\n\\]\nThe same inequality holds at \\(v=0\\). The right-hand side is the support function of the compact convex set\n\\[\nD_0+(s-p)Q,\n\\]\nwith \\(0Q=\\{0\\}\\). Convex separation therefore gives\n\\[\nx+y\\in D_0+(s-p)Q.\n\\]</p><p>Choose a corresponding pair \\(r_+,r_-\\), and put\n\\[\nm=x-r_+=y+r_-.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n2m=x+y-(r_+-r_-),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\|m\\|\\le c,\\qquad\n\\|x-m\\|\\le A_+,\\qquad\n\\|y-m\\|\\le A_-.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality forces these bounds to be equalities:\n\\[\np_1\n\\le\n\\|m\\|+\\|x-m\\|\n\\le\nc+A_+=p_1,\n\\]\n\\[\np_2\n\\le\n\\|m\\|+\\|y-m\\|\n\\le\nc+A_-=p_2.\n\\]\nThus \\(m\\) lies between \\(0,x\\) and between \\(0,y\\), and\n\\[\n\\|x-m\\|+\\|m-y\\|\n=\nA_++A_-=p\n=\n\\|x-y\\|.\n\\]\nThis is the median property. The argument permits \\(c=0\\) or either \\(A_\\pm=0\\), so saturated triangle inequalities require no limiting argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 8.4, “Classification of equality.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(K=-K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), be a convex body satisfying\n\\[\nP(K)=\\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The body \\(K\\) is a linear Hanner body.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(Q=K^\\circ\\). Proposition 8 and Lemma 9.1 give the \\(3.2\\) intersection property for\n\\[\n(\\mathbb R^n,p_Q).\n\\]\nThis is a real Banach space because every finite-dimensional normed space is complete.</p><p>Lemma 9.2 supplies an invertible linear map \\(T\\) and a Hanner polytope \\(H\\) such that\n\\[\nQ=TH.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1,\n\\[\nK=Q^\\circ=T^{-T}H^\\circ\n\\]\nis a linear Hanner body.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, “From medians to three balls.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In this lemma, the setting is an arbitrary real normed space, rather than specifically the finite-dimensional equality body of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume every triple of points has a metric median.</p><p class=\"li\">The three-ball intersection property, also called the \\(3.2\\) intersection property, means that any three pairwise-intersecting closed balls have a common point. Their radii may differ.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space has the three-ball intersection property, including balls of radius zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider pairwise-intersecting balls with centers \\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\) and radii \\(r_1,r_2,r_3\\ge0\\). Let \\(m\\) be a metric median of their centers, and set\n\\[\nd_i=\\|m-x_i\\|.\n\\]\nPairwise intersection and the median property imply\n\\[\nd_i+d_j\n=\n\\|x_i-x_j\\|\n\\le\nr_i+r_j\n\\qquad(i\\ne j).\n\\]</p><p>If every \\(d_i\\le r_i\\), then \\(m\\) is a common point. Otherwise, after relabeling,\n\\[\nt=d_1-r_1&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(j=2,3\\), the displayed inequalities give\n\\[\nd_j+t\\le r_j,\n\\]\nso no other ball excludes \\(m\\). Since \\(0&lt;t\\le d_1\\), the point\n\\[\nz=m+\\frac{t}{d_1}(x_1-m)\n\\]\nis well-defined and lies on the segment from \\(m\\) to \\(x_1\\). It satisfies\n\\[\n\\|z-x_1\\|=d_1-t=r_1,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|z-x_j\\|\n\\le\n\\|z-m\\|+d_j\n=\nt+d_j\n\\le r_j\n\\qquad(j=2,3).\n\\]\nThus \\(z\\) lies in all three balls. This includes \\(r_1=0\\), when \\(z=x_1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Theorem 8.3, “Hansen–Lima.” This is an external structure theorem invoked by the paper", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(E\\) be a nonzero finite-dimensional real Banach space with the \\(3.2\\) intersection property: any three pairwise-intersecting closed balls, with arbitrary radii, have a common point.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(E\\) is linearly isometric to a space obtained from the real line by finitely many \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_\\infty\\) direct sums.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Hansen–Lima, “The structure of finite dimensional Banach spaces with the \\(3.2\\). intersection property,” Acta Mathematica \\(146\\) (1981), Corollary 7.4, cited as reference [13] in the paper. The paper invokes this result without proving it.</p><p>The two direct-sum norms are\n\\[\np_{A\\oplus_1A'}(x,x')=p_A(x)+p_{A'}(x'),\n\\]\n\\[\np_{A\\oplus_\\infty A'}(x,x')\n=\n\\max\\{p_A(x),p_{A'}(x')\\},\n\\]\nso their recursively constructed unit balls are Hanner polytopes.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [7, 15, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [15, 2, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"]]});
